title: The Crédit Mobilier Scandal
subtitle: An October Surprise
tags: event
view-if: year = 1872 and month = 10
max-visits: 1
on-arrival: {!
    Q.liberal_dissent += 6;
    Q.moderate_dissent += 4;
    Q.conservative_dissent += 3;
    if (Q.radical_split == 1) {
        Q.radical_relation -= 3;
    } else {
        Q.radical_dissent += 3;
    }
    Q.middle_class_dem += 4;
    Q.workers_dem += 2;
    Q.rural_dem += 2;
    Q.dem_relation -= 6;
    Q.dem_reform_strength += 8;
!}
face-image: img/Keppler_Credit_Mobilier_Hari-Kari.png

= The Credit Mobilier Scandal

The morning papers hit Washington hard. Documents have surfaced exposing a scheme tied to Credit Mobilier, the construction company responsible for much of the Union Pacific Railroad. The company charged the railroad far more than the actual cost of construction and used the proceeds to enrich its officers and associates.

To discourage congressional scrutiny, discounted Credit Mobilier shares were distributed to several members of Congress. Prominent <span style="color: #7A1F5C;">**Radicals**</span> and <span style="color: #9D2933;">**Moderates**</span> are implicated.

[? if vice_president = "Colfax" : Worse still, Vice President Schuyler Colfax is among those named. He is accused of receiving shares while serving as Speaker of the House. Colfax denies wrongdoing, but the evidence has already damaged his defense. ?]

[? if liberal_split = 1 : The <span style="color: #DAA520;">**Liberal Republicans**</span> call the scandal proof of the corruption they have warned about for years. The <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democrats**</span> are making the same argument from the other side of the aisle. ?]

[? if radical_split = 1 : Some of the men implicated sit with the breakaway <span style="color: #7A1F5C;">**Radical Republican Party**</span> rather than the regular organization, an awkward complication for a party that has spent the last few years positioning itself as the more principled wing of the movement. ?]

How do we respond?

- @cover_up: Dismiss the reporting and hold the line.
- @sacrifice: Cut loose the implicated members and distance the administration from the scandal.
- @sacrifice_vp: Cut loose the implicated members and push Colfax out.
- @independent_investigation: Support a full independent investigation.

@cover_up
on-arrival: {!
    Q.liberal_dissent += 8;
    Q.moderate_dissent += 3;
    Q.conservative_dissent -= 2;
    Q.middle_class_dem += 4;
    Q.middle_class_rep -= 3;
    Q.workers_dem += 2;
    Q.rural_dem += 2;
    Q.dem_relation -= 4;
    Q.dem_reform_strength += 7;
!}

We instruct party newspapers to dismiss the story as a partisan attack timed to damage us before the election. Friendly editors question the documents and the motives of those who published them. The implicated members are told to keep quiet.

For the moment, the damage is contained. Our core supporters remain with us and the campaign continues. Inside the party, the reaction is harsher. Reformers who joined the <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republicans**</span> to oppose corruption now see the party defending it. The <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democrats**</span> have an accusation that is difficult to dismiss when the documents themselves are real.

[? if liberal_split = 1 : The <span style="color: #DAA520;">**Liberal Republicans**</span> are using this in every speech and pamphlet between now and November. ?]

[? if radical_split = 1 : The breakaway <span style="color: #7A1F5C;">**Radical Republican Party**</span> is doing the same, careful to draw a line between its own implicated members and the "regular organization men" it says never should have been trusted with the railroad in the first place. ?]

@sacrifice
on-arrival: {!
    Q.liberal_dissent -= 3;
    Q.moderate_dissent -= 2;
    Q.conservative_dissent += 4;
    if (Q.radical_split == 1) {
        Q.radical_relation -= 3;
    } else {
        Q.radical_dissent += 3;
    }
    Q.middle_class_rep += 2;
    Q.middle_class_dem += 2;
    Q.dem_relation += 3;
    Q.resources -= 1;
!}

We move quickly. Several of the most visibly implicated members are encouraged to step away from public office or decline to seek reelection. The administration calls for a full accounting rather than waiting for the <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democrats**</span> to set the terms of the debate.

Reformers give us some credit for acting. The men being cut loose and their allies are less forgiving.

[? if radical_split = 0 : <span style="color: #B5651D;">**Conservatives**</span> and <span style="color: #7A1F5C;">**Radicals**</span> who depended on those members resent the decision. ?]

[? if radical_split = 1 : <span style="color: #B5651D;">**Conservatives**</span> who depended on those members resent the decision, and the breakaway <span style="color: #7A1F5C;">**Radical Republican Party**</span> calls it a half-measure meant to save the organization rather than clean it out. ?]

The scandal still hurts, but the party has shown some willingness to discipline its own.

@sacrifice_vp
view-if: vice_president = "Colfax"
on-arrival: {!
    Q.liberal_dissent -= 5;
    Q.moderate_dissent += 2;
    Q.conservative_dissent += 3;
    if (Q.radical_split == 1) {
        Q.radical_relation -= 2;
    } else {
        Q.radical_dissent += 2;
    }
    Q.middle_class_rep += 3;
    Q.dem_relation += 4;
    Q.resources -= 1;
!}
go-to: kick_out

We cut loose the implicated members, but Schuyler Colfax presents a larger problem. His denials have failed to settle the matter and his continued presence on the ticket is becoming a liability. We begin the process of pushing him out.

@independent_investigation
choose-if: budget >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: You don't have the Budget for this
on-arrival: {!
    Q.liberal_dissent -= 6;
    Q.liberal_strength += 3;
    Q.moderate_dissent -= 3;
    Q.conservative_dissent += 6;
    if (Q.radical_split == 1) {
        Q.radical_relation -= 4;
    } else {
        Q.radical_dissent += 4;
    }
    Q.middle_class_rep += 5;
    Q.middle_class_dem -= 2;
    Q.dem_relation += 4;
    Q.budget -= 1;
!}

We call for an immediate independent congressional investigation with full subpoena power. The administration acknowledges that the documents appear credible and that Congress must be allowed to examine the allegations without interference.

The reformist wing approves. Members who had been drifting toward the <span style="color: #DAA520;">**Liberal Republicans**</span> have less reason to leave. The <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democrats**</span> can still attack us, but a party that has opened its own books is harder to portray as completely corrupt.

The investigation will hurt. Several prominent <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republicans**</span> will come under scrutiny, including men who expected the party to protect them.

[? if radical_split = 0 : <span style="color: #B5651D;">**Conservatives**</span> and <span style="color: #7A1F5C;">**Radicals**</span> alike resent the exposure. ?]

[? if radical_split = 1 : <span style="color: #B5651D;">**Conservatives**</span> resent the exposure, and the breakaway <span style="color: #7A1F5C;">**Radical Republican Party**</span> resents it just as much, having no wish to see its own implicated members dragged before the same committee. ?]

@kick_out

The Vice President must go. The question is how?

- @Dropped: Have the President pressure Colfax to step aside.
- @Slap: Give Colfax a slap on the wrist for the media

@Dropped
on-arrival: {!
    Q.vice_president = "";
    Q.liberal_dissent -= 4;
    Q.moderate_dissent -= 2;
    Q.conservative_dissent += 5;
    if (Q.radical_split == 1) {
        Q.radical_relation -= 3;
    } else {
        Q.radical_dissent += 3;
    }
    Q.middle_class_rep += 4;
    Q.freemen_rep += 1;
    Q.dem_relation += 5;
!}

The President makes clear through intermediaries that Colfax cannot remain in office. Colfax resists briefly, then agrees to step aside. His statement cites his health and the demands of the office. Few believe it.

The scandal's most visible figure is gone. We can at least claim that the party acted when it mattered.

Colfax's <span style="color: #B5651D;">**Conservative**</span> allies are furious. They accuse the administration of sacrificing a loyal party man to protect itself.

@Slap
on-arrival: {!
    Q.liberal_dissent += 5;
    Q.moderate_dissent += 2;
    Q.conservative_dissent -= 3;
    Q.middle_class_dem += 3;
    Q.middle_class_rep -= 2;
    Q.dem_relation -= 2;
!}

We issue a statement expressing disappointment in Colfax's conduct while allowing him to remain in office. He is advised to apologize publicly and reduce his campaign appearances.

Reformers see the decision as protection for a corrupt official. The <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democratic**</span> press makes the same charge, arguing that the party values victory over accountability.

Colfax remains on the ticket, and the scandal follows him through the campaign.